{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0152uPPCcGKwqio1AVLKC3S9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# warmups — technique log (days 1-9; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n7\t- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13, sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal, \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL percent-encoding, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML character entities, \"characters would like to escape\"/\"Escape Room\" -> JSON/JS `\\uXXXX` string escapes. Every single_solve so far was ONE layer.\n8\t- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 38, 43). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, in that order so far). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n9\t- Clocks: TT about 5-8 s from day 2 on (30 s on day 1), mastery about 5-9 s (9 s on days 7 and 9, 8 s on day 8: the window does not reliably grow with the set count). Only a deterministic solver survives these windows. The single_solve is untimed.\n10\t\n11\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n12\t- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, `&#NNN;`\n13\t  entities, `\"\\uXXXX\"` JSON escapes, ...); detect each line's encoding from its alphabet, not its position (position-based detection breaks if the order\n14\t  changes). Rule for \"escape-token\" encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind\n15\t  (`re.sub(token,'',frag).strip()==''`), then use the stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) so the unseen forms\n16\t  (`%XX` upper, `&#x6d;` hex, `&amp;` named) are covered too. Day 8 GM bytes used only decimal `&#NNN;` in all 40 blobs.\n17\t  Day 9 backslash escapes: every char as lowercase `\\u00XX`, the fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (a JSON string\n18\t  literal) in all 45 blobs (single, TT, 43 mastery lines); no `\\x`, `\\n`, `\\\"`, uppercase hex or non-ASCII seen. Rule: if\n19\t  the fragment matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, decode with `json.loads` (add the quotes if missing) -- that also covers\n20\t  `\\n`, `\\\"`, surrogate pairs and non-ASCII; fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`. Drop the quotes.\n21\t- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n22\t- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day (33 on day 7, 38 on day 8, 43 on day 9); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists.\n23\t- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n24\t  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n25\t- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n26\t- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n27\t\n28\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n29\t| What you see | First move |\n30\t|---|---|\n31\t| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n32\t| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n33\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n34\t| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n35\t| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n36\t| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n37\t| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n38\t| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n39\t| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n40\t| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n41\t| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n42\t| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n43\t| Magic `1f8b`/`78`/`BZh`/`fd377a58` | gunzip / zlib / bzip2 / xz (in the toolbox. Not seen in the GM bytes yet) |\n44\t| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\n45\tThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n46\t\n47\t## Day by day\n48\t**Day 1: A Little Based** (\"sixty-four is a good base\").\n49\t- Artifact: 148-byte base64, one layer, decoded with `base64 -d` -> slug. CORRECT on the first submit.\n50\t- The first 6 worker launches died in under 8 s with \"OAuth session expired\" (harness auth, not the challenge). The successful worker took about 95 s.\n51\t- TT and mastery windows were 30 s. Mastery: 3 sets, each blob ONE line `01\\t<base64>`.\n52\t- Mastery failed about 12 times in a row. The cause was the HARNESS, not the decode: the contract listed 3 downloads all named `blob.txt`, and `hctf artifact` saved only set1 (see challenges/day01/warmups-a-little-based/HELP.md). The solver now matches files by the contract's `artifacts[].sha256`.\n53\t\n54\t**Day 2: Half a Turn** (\"Thirteen steps forward\").\n55\t- 108-byte ROT13 text -> `tr` rot13 -> slug. CORRECT first submit. Worker about 124 s, mostly harness bookkeeping. One stale start.json, overwritten by a re-start with no attempt_id, broke the completion check. It was restored from log.jsonl.\n56\t- TT window 5 s. Several TT submits came back `expired` before the solver became the fast path.\n57\t- Mastery 8 sets: a single download, a ZIP named `blob-01.txt` holding `blob-01..08.txt`. Each blob is 2 lines: base64, then rot13, joined verbatim.\n58\t\n59\t**Day 3: Hex Appeal** (\"Sixteen ... fingers\").\n60\t- Plain hex string -> `xxd -r -p`. The single_solve took seconds via the reused generic solver (no worker log).\n61\t- Mastery 13 sets x 3 lines (b64 / rot13 / hex), window 7 s.\n62\t- Live mastery TIMEOUT: `english()` counted single letters, so a hex line (\"a\", \"e\" heavy) scored as English and was never decoded. The BFS took about 32 s per blob. Fixes: score only words of 2+ letters, add the fast `strict_line` path (strict hex -> strict b64 -> best rot-N, about 0.2 s for 13 sets), and cap the peel BFS at 0.6 s per question.\n63\t\n64\t**Day 4: Two of a Kind** (\"only two sides\").\n65\t- Space-separated 8-bit binary. Reused solver, seconds.\n66\t- Mastery 18 sets x 4 lines (+ binary line), window 7 s. Try binary before hex: 8-bit 0/1 groups are also valid hex.\n67\t- Bug: the slug fragment \"er: <slug>\" got rot-shifted to \"an: ...\" because \"an\" is a common word. Rule now: a rot-N result must beat the UNSHIFTED text by >= 2.0 English score.\n68\t\n69\t**Day 5: By the Numbers** (\"reduced to numbers\").\n70\t- Space-separated decimal ASCII codes. Reused solver, single_solve start->submit about 7 s per log.jsonl.\n71\t- Mastery 23 sets x 5 lines (+ decimal), zip bundle again. TT window 6 s, mastery 7 s. CORRECT on attempt 1.\n72\t\n73\t**Day 6: Eight Is Enough** (\"counted on eight fingers. Again.\").\n74\t- 109 zero-padded 3-digit octal codes, ONE layer. \"Again\" was NOT a second octal layer.\n75\t- Worker about 355 s. Decoded in the first minute, the rest went to solver hardening.\n76\t- Near-miss: a shell `while read` loop dropped the last token (the file has NO trailing newline), giving `...-3`. Checking against the \"two-digit number\" syntax caught it before submit.\n77\t- Mastery 28 sets x 6 lines (+ octal), zip bundle. Octal and decimal both parse `101 110`. Tells: zero padding (`040`) means octal, a short unpadded token (`32`) means decimal. Decimal codes misread as octal come out ALL CAPS, so lowercase share helps the decision. Slug-only rot13 fragments are a near-tie on English score: in numbered blobs, give rot13 a prior.\n78\t\n79\t**Day 7: Percent Chance** (\"My address bar is having a moment\").\n80\t- 318-byte blob, every byte percent-encoded (`%4e%69...`), ONE layer -> `urllib.parse.unquote` -> slug. Start->CORRECT 53 s, first submit.\n81\t- Worker wrote the solver from the harness skeleton (alphabet-detected `decode_frag`: `%xx` -> binary -> dec/oct -> hex -> b64 -> rot13).\n82\t- TT window 7 s, CORRECT at 2.4 s. Mastery 33 sets x 7 lines (+ url line), window 9 s.\n83\t- Mastery attempt 1 failed: set1 resolved to the WHOLE zip (bundle saved as `blob-01.txt`, a name match returned the outer file), so unquote over all 33 members gave another set's slug fragment (`py-storm-dune-80` from set 27's `zippy-...`) -> incorrect; set26 then `expired`. After `lib.common.find_artifact` was patched to return the same-named member, attempt 2 was CORRECT (7.1 s of 9 s; the start call alone took 2.9 s).\n84\t\n85\t**Day 8: Entity Relations** (\"The browser understands. Do you?\" -- HTML character entities, what a browser renders).\n86\t- LIB (ratchet D8): `lib.decode.decode_chunk` now has a strict whole-fragment HTML-entity branch (decimal/hex/named);\n87\t  `decode_chunked` alone reproduces all 38 D8 mastery slugs -- import it, don't re-type the entity regex.\n88\t- 620-byte blob of decimal HTML entities only (`&#78;&#105;&#99;...`, no line end), ONE layer -> `html.unescape` -> slug.\n89\t  Triage's deterministic preview already said \"riddle -> HTML character entities\".\n90\t- A pre-warmed worker made 2 tool calls: `html.unescape` plus a `lib.decode.decode_chunk` cross-check (it also handles entities,\n91\t  via its generic fallback), then patched the solver and submitted. Start (15:43:54Z) -> CORRECT (15:44:12Z) about 18 s, first submit.\n92\t- Solver: if a fragment is all `&...;` tokens -> `html.unescape`, else `decode_chunk`. TT window 8 s, CORRECT at 2.6 s.\n93\t  Mastery 38 sets x 8 lines (+ entity line), zip bundle, window 8 s, CORRECT at 1.6 s (solve 0.2 s). Both on attempt 1.\n94\t- Near-miss: the worker's in-place `str.replace` patch for `fallback()` silently matched nothing (the docstring wrapping\n95\t  differed), so the fallback stayed `return None`. It didn't matter here, but check that each patch actually applied.\n96\t\n97\t**Day 9: Escape Room** (\"These characters would very much like to escape.\" -- backslash/unicode escape sequences).\n98\t- 644-byte blob: ONE JSON string literal, `\"` + 107 x `\\u00XX` (lowercase hex, every char incl. spaces) + `\"`, no line end.\n99\t  One layer -> `json.loads` -> sentence -> slug. `lib.decode.decode_chunk(text.strip())` already returned the plain sentence\n100\t  (its generic `\\u/\\x` escape fallback keeps only the escapes, so the quotes vanish).\n101\t- Pre-warmed worker: 3 tool calls (read triage/contract/skeleton; `decode_chunk` + `json.loads` cross-check; patch `answer()`\n102\t  and `hctf submit --from-solver` in one command). Start 13:00:06Z -> CORRECT 13:00:36Z, about 30 s, first submit.\n103\t- Solver: `decode_chunked` for `NN<TAB>` blobs else `decode_chunk`, then the last-line slug regex. Its `json.loads`\n104\t  post-step never fired (lib had already unescaped).\n105\t- TT window 7 s, CORRECT at 0.8 s after start (whole run 1.6 s). Mastery 43 sets x 9 lines (+ quoted `\\u` line), zip\n106\t  bundle as `blob-01.txt`, window 9 s, CORRECT at 0.9 s (solve 0.3 s). Both on attempt 1, 43/43 slugs accepted as decoded.\n107\t- Gap: lib's `\\u` path is ASCII-only (`_ascii`); a non-ASCII escape (`\\u00e9`) makes the whole fragment fall through\n108\t  to rot-N garbage, and `\\n`/`\\\"`/surrogate pairs are dropped. Not in the GM bytes yet.\n109\t\n110\t## GM answer conventions learned\n111\t- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n112\t- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-9; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n113\t- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n114\t- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n115\t\n116\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n117\t- Mastery bytes missing: duplicate `blob.txt` downloads overwrote each other or were never fetched (day 1, about 12 failed attempts). If a set's bytes aren't on disk, it is a downloader problem. Don't spawn solver-hardening workers.\n118\t- A `.txt` file that is really a ZIP (check the magic, not the extension). Prefer the exact inner member over the outer same-named container. Day 7 mastery lost an attempt to this: set 1 decoded the whole bundle. A slug that is a word suffix (`py-...`), or a set1 answer that doesn't come from its own last line, is the tell. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the zip-named-`blob-01.txt` layout, so test on the raw delivery.\n119\t- English scoring: count single letters and hex reads as English (day 3 timeout). Score common 2-letter words without a margin and a correct line gets rot-shifted (day 4). Require a margin over the unshifted text.\n120\t- Encoding confusion: binary is also valid hex (try binary first), and decimal is also valid octal (use padding and case).\n121\t- Fragments are split mid-word. Join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace. Joining with spaces corrupts the sentence and the slug.\n122\t- No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Use python or `tr ' ' '\\n'` plus an explicit newline. Always regex-check the slug against the answer syntax.\n123\t- Timed windows (5-9 s, with GM start/submit eating 3-5 s of it) leave no room for an LLM or a slow BFS. Keep every per-question decode under about 1 s with a hard BFS cap.\n124\t- Solver patches made with `str.replace`/sed silently no-op when the anchor text doesn't match exactly (day 8 fallback). Assert\n125\t  the anchor count or re-grep the file after patching.\n126\t- Hand-testing escape decoders: in a ratchet session, a literal backslash-u sequence typed into a Bash/heredoc command\n127\t  reached python ALREADY decoded (the tool layer unescaped it), so a test \"passed\" on plain text. Test on the artifact file\n128\t  or build escapes with `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n129\t- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n130\t- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n131\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b91bdd74-729a-4aee-94d0-8b48c90d173c","uuid":"36ce2ad8-0e1e-405e-b955-7d89fc606638","timestamp":"2026-10-12T02:00:50.935Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/warmups.md","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-9; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13, sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal, \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL percent-encoding, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML character entities, \"characters would like to escape\"/\"Escape Room\" -> JSON/JS `\\uXXXX` string escapes. Every single_solve so far was ONE layer.\n- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 38, 43). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, in that order so far). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n- Clocks: TT about 5-8 s from day 2 on (30 s on day 1), mastery about 5-9 s (9 s on days 7 and 9, 8 s on day 8: the window does not reliably grow with the set count). Only a deterministic solver survives these windows. The single_solve is untimed.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, `&#NNN;`\n  entities, `\"\\uXXXX\"` JSON escapes, ...); detect each line's encoding from its alphabet, not its position (position-based detection breaks if the order\n  changes). Rule for \"escape-token\" encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind\n  (`re.sub(token,'',frag).strip()==''`), then use the stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) so the unseen forms\n  (`%XX` upper, `&#x6d;` hex, `&amp;` named) are covered too. Day 8 GM bytes used only decimal `&#NNN;` in all 40 blobs.\n  Day 9 backslash escapes: every char as lowercase `\\u00XX`, the fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (a JSON string\n  literal) in all 45 blobs (single, TT, 43 mastery lines); no `\\x`, `\\n`, `\\\"`, uppercase hex or non-ASCII seen. Rule: if\n  the fragment matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, decode with `json.loads` (add the quotes if missing) -- that also covers\n  `\\n`, `\\\"`, surrogate pairs and non-ASCII; fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`. Drop the quotes.\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day (33 on day 7, 38 on day 8, 43 on day 9); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| Magic `1f8b`/`78`/`BZh`/`fd377a58` | gunzip / zlib / bzip2 / xz (in the toolbox. Not seen in the GM bytes yet) |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Day 1: A Little Based** (\"sixty-four is a good base\").\n- Artifact: 148-byte base64, one layer, decoded with `base64 -d` -> slug. CORRECT on the first submit.\n- The first 6 worker launches died in under 8 s with \"OAuth session expired\" (harness auth, not the challenge). The successful worker took about 95 s.\n- TT and mastery windows were 30 s. Mastery: 3 sets, each blob ONE line `01\\t<base64>`.\n- Mastery failed about 12 times in a row. The cause was the HARNESS, not the decode: the contract listed 3 downloads all named `blob.txt`, and `hctf artifact` saved only set1 (see challenges/day01/warmups-a-little-based/HELP.md). The solver now matches files by the contract's `artifacts[].sha256`.\n\n**Day 2: Half a Turn** (\"Thirteen steps forward\").\n- 108-byte ROT13 text -> `tr` rot13 -> slug. CORRECT first submit. Worker about 124 s, mostly harness bookkeeping. One stale start.json, overwritten by a re-start with no attempt_id, broke the completion check. It was restored from log.jsonl.\n- TT window 5 s. Several TT submits came back `expired` before the solver became the fast path.\n- Mastery 8 sets: a single download, a ZIP named `blob-01.txt` holding `blob-01..08.txt`. Each blob is 2 lines: base64, then rot13, joined verbatim.\n\n**Day 3: Hex Appeal** (\"Sixteen ... fingers\").\n- Plain hex string -> `xxd -r -p`. The single_solve took seconds via the reused generic solver (no worker log).\n- Mastery 13 sets x 3 lines (b64 / rot13 / hex), window 7 s.\n- Live mastery TIMEOUT: `english()` counted single letters, so a hex line (\"a\", \"e\" heavy) scored as English and was never decoded. The BFS took about 32 s per blob. Fixes: score only words of 2+ letters, add the fast `strict_line` path (strict hex -> strict b64 -> best rot-N, about 0.2 s for 13 sets), and cap the peel BFS at 0.6 s per question.\n\n**Day 4: Two of a Kind** (\"only two sides\").\n- Space-separated 8-bit binary. Reused solver, seconds.\n- Mastery 18 sets x 4 lines (+ binary line), window 7 s. Try binary before hex: 8-bit 0/1 groups are also valid hex.\n- Bug: the slug fragment \"er: <slug>\" got rot-shifted to \"an: ...\" because \"an\" is a common word. Rule now: a rot-N result must beat the UNSHIFTED text by >= 2.0 English score.\n\n**Day 5: By the Numbers** (\"reduced to numbers\").\n- Space-separated decimal ASCII codes. Reused solver, single_solve start->submit about 7 s per log.jsonl.\n- Mastery 23 sets x 5 lines (+ decimal), zip bundle again. TT window 6 s, mastery 7 s. CORRECT on attempt 1.\n\n**Day 6: Eight Is Enough** (\"counted on eight fingers. Again.\").\n- 109 zero-padded 3-digit octal codes, ONE layer. \"Again\" was NOT a second octal layer.\n- Worker about 355 s. Decoded in the first minute, the rest went to solver hardening.\n- Near-miss: a shell `while read` loop dropped the last token (the file has NO trailing newline), giving `...-3`. Checking against the \"two-digit number\" syntax caught it before submit.\n- Mastery 28 sets x 6 lines (+ octal), zip bundle. Octal and decimal both parse `101 110`. Tells: zero padding (`040`) means octal, a short unpadded token (`32`) means decimal. Decimal codes misread as octal come out ALL CAPS, so lowercase share helps the decision. Slug-only rot13 fragments are a near-tie on English score: in numbered blobs, give rot13 a prior.\n\n**Day 7: Percent Chance** (\"My address bar is having a moment\").\n- 318-byte blob, every byte percent-encoded (`%4e%69...`), ONE layer -> `urllib.parse.unquote` -> slug. Start->CORRECT 53 s, first submit.\n- Worker wrote the solver from the harness skeleton (alphabet-detected `decode_frag`: `%xx` -> binary -> dec/oct -> hex -> b64 -> rot13).\n- TT window 7 s, CORRECT at 2.4 s. Mastery 33 sets x 7 lines (+ url line), window 9 s.\n- Mastery attempt 1 failed: set1 resolved to the WHOLE zip (bundle saved as `blob-01.txt`, a name match returned the outer file), so unquote over all 33 members gave another set's slug fragment (`py-storm-dune-80` from set 27's `zippy-...`) -> incorrect; set26 then `expired`. After `lib.common.find_artifact` was patched to return the same-named member, attempt 2 was CORRECT (7.1 s of 9 s; the start call alone took 2.9 s).\n\n**Day 8: Entity Relations** (\"The browser understands. Do you?\" -- HTML character entities, what a browser renders).\n- LIB (ratchet D8): `lib.decode.decode_chunk` now has a strict whole-fragment HTML-entity branch (decimal/hex/named);\n  `decode_chunked` alone reproduces all 38 D8 mastery slugs -- import it, don't re-type the entity regex.\n- 620-byte blob of decimal HTML entities only (`&#78;&#105;&#99;...`, no line end), ONE layer -> `html.unescape` -> slug.\n  Triage's deterministic preview already said \"riddle -> HTML character entities\".\n- A pre-warmed worker made 2 tool calls: `html.unescape` plus a `lib.decode.decode_chunk` cross-check (it also handles entities,\n  via its generic fallback), then patched the solver and submitted. Start (15:43:54Z) -> CORRECT (15:44:12Z) about 18 s, first submit.\n- Solver: if a fragment is all `&...;` tokens -> `html.unescape`, else `decode_chunk`. TT window 8 s, CORRECT at 2.6 s.\n  Mastery 38 sets x 8 lines (+ entity line), zip bundle, window 8 s, CORRECT at 1.6 s (solve 0.2 s). Both on attempt 1.\n- Near-miss: the worker's in-place `str.replace` patch for `fallback()` silently matched nothing (the docstring wrapping\n  differed), so the fallback stayed `return None`. It didn't matter here, but check that each patch actually applied.\n\n**Day 9: Escape Room** (\"These characters would very much like to escape.\" -- backslash/unicode escape sequences).\n- 644-byte blob: ONE JSON string literal, `\"` + 107 x `\\u00XX` (lowercase hex, every char incl. spaces) + `\"`, no line end.\n  One layer -> `json.loads` -> sentence -> slug. `lib.decode.decode_chunk(text.strip())` already returned the plain sentence\n  (its generic `\\u/\\x` escape fallback keeps only the escapes, so the quotes vanish).\n- Pre-warmed worker: 3 tool calls (read triage/contract/skeleton; `decode_chunk` + `json.loads` cross-check; patch `answer()`\n  and `hctf submit --from-solver` in one command). Start 13:00:06Z -> CORRECT 13:00:36Z, about 30 s, first submit.\n- Solver: `decode_chunked` for `NN<TAB>` blobs else `decode_chunk`, then the last-line slug regex. Its `json.loads`\n  post-step never fired (lib had already unescaped).\n- TT window 7 s, CORRECT at 0.8 s after start (whole run 1.6 s). Mastery 43 sets x 9 lines (+ quoted `\\u` line), zip\n  bundle as `blob-01.txt`, window 9 s, CORRECT at 0.9 s (solve 0.3 s). Both on attempt 1, 43/43 slugs accepted as decoded.\n- Gap: lib's `\\u` path is ASCII-only (`_ascii`); a non-ASCII escape (`\\u00e9`) makes the whole fragment fall through\n  to rot-N garbage, and `\\n`/`\\\"`/surrogate pairs are dropped. Not in the GM bytes yet.\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-9; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- Mastery bytes missing: duplicate `blob.txt` downloads overwrote each other or were never fetched (day 1, about 12 failed attempts). If a set's bytes aren't on disk, it is a downloader problem. Don't spawn solver-hardening workers.\n- A `.txt` file that is really a ZIP (check the magic, not the extension). Prefer the exact inner member over the outer same-named container. Day 7 mastery lost an attempt to this: set 1 decoded the whole bundle. A slug that is a word suffix (`py-...`), or a set1 answer that doesn't come from its own last line, is the tell. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the zip-named-`blob-01.txt` layout, so test on the raw delivery.\n- English scoring: count single letters and hex reads as English (day 3 timeout). Score common 2-letter words without a margin and a correct line gets rot-shifted (day 4). Require a margin over the unshifted text.\n- Encoding confusion: binary is also valid hex (try binary first), and decimal is also valid octal (use padding and case).\n- Fragments are split mid-word. Join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace. Joining with spaces corrupts the sentence and the slug.\n- No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Use python or `tr ' ' '\\n'` plus an explicit newline. Always regex-check the slug against the answer syntax.\n- Timed windows (5-9 s, with GM start/submit eating 3-5 s of it) leave no room for an LLM or a slow BFS. Keep every per-question decode under about 1 s with a hard BFS cap.\n- Solver patches made with `str.replace`/sed silently no-op when the anchor text doesn't match exactly (day 8 fallback). Assert\n  the anchor count or re-grep the file after patching.\n- Hand-testing escape decoders: in a ratchet session, a literal backslash-u sequence typed into a Bash/heredoc command\n  reached python ALREADY decoded (the tool layer unescaped it), so a test \"passed\" on plain text. Test on the artifact file\n  or build escapes with `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n","numLines":131,"startLine":1,"totalLines":131}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0152uPPCcGKwqio1AVLKC3S9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
